    "excerpt": "Engineering leadership Q&A for standards that keep GenAI systems maintainable. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-engineering-leadership",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:29.186Z",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "content": "## Scope\nReliability and mentoring norms for AI platform teams.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **Leadership Q&A: Mentoring, code reviews, docs, reliability, observability** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Leadership Q&A: Mentoring, code reviews, d))\n    Technical Leadership\n    Team Mentoring\n    Code Reviews\n    Documentation Standards\n    Reliability Engineering\n    Observability\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What should code reviews enforce for GenAI?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nTests/evals present, no secrets/PHI in fixtures, typed interfaces, observability hooks, and rollback plan—not only style nits.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. Documentation standards?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nRunbooks, model/prompt cards, architecture decision records, and onboarding paths that let a new engineer ship a safe change in days.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. Reliability engineering for LLM apps?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nSLOs for availability/latency/quality, error budgets, graceful degradation (retrieve-only / template fallback), and chaos-tested dependencies.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. Observability as leadership?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nMake traces/metrics the shared language in incidents. Blameless reviews that improve harnesses and evals.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. Mentoring move that scales?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nPair on the first production incident and the first eval design—skills that lectures do not transfer.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Self-check\nList your team's Definition of Done for a prompt change.\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nReviews enforce evals, redaction, rollback. Reliability: SLOs, graceful degradation, dependency testing. Observability (Langfuse/MLflow/LangSmith) is the shared incident language. Mentoring transfers incident + eval skills.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nGenAI reliability is a leadership system: reviews, docs, SLOs, and mentoring — not heroics.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Definition of Done includes eval + runbook\n- Error budgets for quality and availability\n- Onboarding path measured in days not months\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Merging prompt changes without tests\n- Incidents that only restart pods and learn nothing\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
