    "excerpt": "Interoperability and privacy Q&A citing HL7 FHIR R4 and HHS HIPAA guidance. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-fhir-hipaa-ehr",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:29.167Z",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "content": "## Scope\n[HL7 FHIR R4](https://hl7.org/fhir/R4/), [HL7](https://www.hl7.org/), [US Core](https://www.hl7.org/fhir/us/core/), [SMART App Launch](https://hl7.org/fhir/smart-app-launch/), [HHS HIPAA](https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/index.html).\n\n\n## Visual overview\nEHR-aware path: auth → FHIR → scoped RAG → human confirmation.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  User[User] --> Auth[SMART-style auth]\n  Auth --> FHIR[FHIR APIs]\n  FHIR --> RAG[Scoped retrieval]\n  RAG --> LLM[Assisted answer]\n  LLM --> Human[Clinician confirm]\n  Human --> Write[Optional write-back]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Healthcare GenAI))\n    Standards\n      FHIR R4\n      HL7\n      US Core\n    Privacy\n      PHI\n      HIPAA\n      Audit\n    Safety\n      HITL\n      Intended use\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What is Medical NLP?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nNLP on clinical text: sectioning, NER (problems/meds), coding assistance, summarization. High-stakes: require clinician review pathways and strong evaluation on clinical corpora.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. HL7 vs FHIR?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nHL7 is the standards organization (and legacy v2 messaging). **FHIR** is HL7's modern healthcare API/resource standard widely used for EHR integration. US regulation references FHIR R4 and US Core IGs for certified APIs.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What EHR-aware GenAI must respect?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nPatient context scoping, SMART-on-FHIR style auth patterns where applicable, audit, and non-interruptive UX. Prefer FHIR APIs over scraping chart UIs.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. HIPAA-minded GenAI controls?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nMinimum necessary, BAAs for vendors, encryption, access controls, audit logs, and no unconstrained PHI in third-party prompts without review.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. PHI security in RAG?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nIndex per tenant/patient ACL; redact logs; separate de-identified research indexes from production PHI indexes.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Official references\n- [FHIR R4](https://hl7.org/fhir/R4/) · [SMART App Launch](https://hl7.org/fhir/smart-app-launch/) · [HHS HIPAA](https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/index.html)\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nHL7 FHIR R4 (v4.0.1 Mixed Normative/STU) remains widely used and permanently hosted; current FHIR version is newer (5.0.0)—confirm your EHR's supported version. Resources + references + extensions; REST common but not mandatory; CapabilityStatement/StructureDefinition for interfaces/profiles. Prefer FHIR APIs over UI scraping. HIPAA Privacy/Security Rules still apply to GenAI contexts that touch PHI.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nHealthcare GenAI without FHIR/ACL design becomes unsafe chart chat.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Map user stories to FHIR resources\n- Confirm R4 vs R5 (or IG) support with clinical IT\n- Human confirm before write-back\n- Separate de-id research indexes from PHI prod indexes\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Cross-patient retrieval bugs\n- Auto-ordering from model suggestions\n- Assuming every system is on the latest FHIR major version\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
