    "content": "## Scope\nMLOps/LLMOps delivery practice on top of standard DevOps.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **DevOps Q&A: CI/CD, DevOps, MLOps, and GPU-aware scaling** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((DevOps Q&A: CI/CD, DevOps, MLOps, and GPU-))\n    CI/CD\n    DevOps\n    MLOps\n    GPU-Aware Scaling\n    Cloud Deployment\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. DevOps vs MLOps vs LLMOps?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nDevOps: ship software reliably. MLOps: add data/model artifacts, training pipelines, drift. LLMOps: add prompts, eval suites, token cost, retrieval indexes, and agent traces.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. What belongs in CI for GenAI?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nUnit tests, prompt/eval regression, policy checks (secrets/PHI patterns), image build/scan, and infra plan. CD promotes only after eval gates.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What is GPU-aware scaling?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nAutoscaling that considers GPU utilization, queue depth, and model parallelism—not only CPU. Naive scale-out can explode cost; use caps, scale-to-zero where safe, and batching.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. Hybrid cloud note?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nKeep portable packaging (OCI) and consistent IAM/logging patterns across on-prem/cloud so PHI residency constraints do not fork the whole stack.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Try this\n```text\ncommit → CI eval → build image → GitOps PR → canary → promote/rollback\n```\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nExtend CI/CD with eval suites, prompt regression, and artifact promotion. Ray Serve/K8s GPU scheduling needs utilization/queue signals and hard caps. Airflow can schedule training/reindex/agentic batch jobs. Hybrid cloud needs portable OCI + consistent IAM/logging.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nGPU spend and silent quality regressions are the twin failure modes of GenAI delivery.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Eval gate blocks bad prompts/models\n- Canary + automatic rollback on error/quality burn\n- Cost dashboards next to latency SLOs\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Scale GPUs on CPU metrics only\n- CD without eval — 'ship and see'\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
