    "excerpt": "Infrastructure-as-code and container platform Q&A for LLM/ML deployments. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-containers-iac",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:29.132Z",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "content": "## Scope\n[Docker](https://docs.docker.com/), [Kubernetes](https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/), [Helm](https://helm.sh/docs/), [Terraform](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/docs), [OpenGitOps](https://opengitops.dev/), [CloudFormation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/Welcome.html).\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **DevOps Q&A: Docker, Kubernetes, Helm, Terraform, GitOps, CloudFormation** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((DevOps Q&A: Docker, Kubernetes, Helm, Terr))\n    Docker\n    Kubernetes\n    Helm\n    Terraform\n    GitOps\n    CloudFormation\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. Docker's role in ML/GenAI?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nReproducible runtime for training/serving images. Pin base images and dependency locks; scan for CVEs.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. Why Kubernetes for LLM serving?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nDeclarative deploy, scaling, GPU scheduling, rolling updates, and service discovery. Pair with resource quotas and PodDisruptionBudgets.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. Helm vs raw manifests?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nHelm packages K8s apps as versioned charts—useful for promoting the same LLM service across envs with values overlays.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. Terraform vs CloudFormation?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nTerraform: multi-cloud IaC. CloudFormation: AWS-native stacks. Many orgs use Terraform for cloud resources + Helm/GitOps for cluster apps.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. What is GitOps?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nDesired state in Git; controllers reconcile clusters to that state ([OpenGitOps](https://opengitops.dev/) principles). Ideal for auditable LLM/app promotions.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Official references\n- [Kubernetes](https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/) · [Terraform](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/docs) · [OpenGitOps](https://opengitops.dev/)\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nKubernetes: declarative container platform (service discovery, rollouts, self-healing, secrets, HPA, extensibility)—not a full PaaS; desired-state reconciliation. Docker for images; Helm for charts; Terraform Write→Plan→Apply with state + providers (AWS/Azure/GCP/K8s/Helm); OpenGitOps for Git-reconciled desired state. Distinguish Terraform OSS CLI vs HCP/Enterprise collaboration features.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nLLM services without IaC/GitOps become snowflake GPU boxes.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Immutable image tags promoted across envs\n- GitOps PR required for prod\n- Resource quotas and GPU taints documented\n- Plan approval before Apply in shared environments\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- kubectl apply from laptops as the release process\n- Secrets baked into images\n- Assuming remote state/RBAC exists without HCP/Enterprise or equivalent\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
