    "id": 90,
    "category": "GenAI",
    "title": "Data Q&A: Governance, privacy, quality, PHI, HIPAA, audit, access",
    "tags": [
      "Data Governance",
      "Data Privacy",
      "Data Quality",
      "PHI",
      "HIPAA",
      "Auditability",
      "Access Controls"
    ],
    "excerpt": "Regulated-data Q&A for healthcare/enterprise GenAI—aligned with HHS HIPAA professional guidance. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-data-governance-hipaa",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:29.125Z",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "content": "## Scope\nPrivacy/security practice with [HHS HIPAA for professionals](https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/index.html) as the regulatory north star (engage compliance counsel for legal determinations).\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **Data Q&A: Governance, privacy, quality, PHI, HIPAA, audit, access** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Data Q&A: Governance, privacy, quality, PH))\n    Data Governance\n    Data Privacy\n    Data Quality\n    PHI\n    HIPAA\n    Auditability\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What is data governance?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nOwnership, standards, stewardship, and decision rights over data assets—catalogs, quality rules, retention, and access policies.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. Privacy vs security?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nPrivacy: lawful/ethical use and minimization. Security: protect confidentiality, integrity, availability. GenAI needs both (what you send to a model + how you protect it).\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What is PHI and why it matters for GenAI?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nProtected Health Information under HIPAA. Minimize PHI in prompts/logs; prefer de-identification; encrypt; restrict vendors via BAAs; audit access.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. What is auditability?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nAbility to reconstruct who accessed what data, which model/prompt version answered, and what context was used—essential for incidents and regulators.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. Access controls baseline?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nLeast privilege IAM, tenant/patient scoping at retrieval, break-glass procedures, and periodic access reviews.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q6. Data quality for ML?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nCompleteness, validity, consistency, timeliness. Quality failures should fail the pipeline, not silently train.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Official references\n- [HHS HIPAA](https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/index.html)\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nHHS HIPAA for Professionals: Privacy Rule protects IIHI for covered entities; Security Rule covers ePHI confidentiality/integrity/availability; Enforcement and Omnibus/HITECH/Breach Notification apply. Prefer 45 C.F.R. Parts 160/162/164 over unofficial combined texts. GenAI multiplies exfiltration via prompts, traces, and vendors—minimum necessary, BAAs, encryption, access reviews, audit.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nOne PHI leak in prompts/logs can end a healthcare GenAI program.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Data classification on every store feeding GenAI\n- Default-deny egress of PHI to model APIs\n- Audit who saw what + which model version answered\n- Vendor BAA + residency review before pilot\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Pasting charts into consumer LLM apps\n- Traces with full clinical notes 'for debugging'\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
