    "id": 89,
    "category": "GenAI",
    "title": "Data Q&A: Lakes, warehouses, Parquet, Delta, lakehouse, feature stores",
    "tags": [
      "Data Lakes",
      "Data Warehouses",
      "Parquet",
      "Delta Lake",
      "Lakehouse",
      "Feature Store"
    ],
    "excerpt": "Storage architecture Q&A for analytics and ML feature consistency. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-lakehouse-feature-store",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:29.118Z",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "content": "## Scope\n[Parquet](https://parquet.apache.org/docs/), [Delta Lake](https://docs.delta.io/latest/index.html), and lakehouse/feature-store patterns.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **Data Q&A: Lakes, warehouses, Parquet, Delta, lakehouse, feature stores** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Data Q&A: Lakes, warehouses, Parquet, Delt))\n    Data Lakes\n    Data Warehouses\n    Parquet\n    Delta Lake\n    Lakehouse\n    Feature Store\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. Lake vs warehouse?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nLakes store broad multi-format data cheaply; warehouses optimize governed SQL analytics. Lakehouse blends lake storage with warehouse reliability (ACID, schema, time travel)—often via Delta/Iceberg/Hudi tables.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. Why Parquet?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nColumnar, compressed, analytics-friendly format. Standard for ML feature extracts and lake tables.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What does Delta Lake add?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nACID transactions, scalable metadata, time travel, and schema enforcement on data lakes (see Delta docs)—critical for reproducible training sets.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. What is a feature store?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nRegistry + serving of features with offline (training) and online (inference) paths, ownership, and point-in-time joins to prevent leakage.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. GenAI note?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nVector indexes are siblings to feature stores—not replacements. Risk scores/routing features still need classical feature pipelines.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Official references\n- [Parquet](https://parquet.apache.org/docs/) · [Delta Lake](https://docs.delta.io/latest/index.html)\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nDelta Lake: ACID on lakes (S3/ADLS/GCS/HDFS), scalable metadata, batch+streaming unification, schema enforcement, time travel, merge/upsert for CDC/SCD, connectors (Spark/Flink/Trino/Athena/…). Parquet columnar storage underneath many lake tables. Feature stores enforce offline/online consistency and PIT joins.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nTrain/serve skew and unreproducible datasets destroy trust in ML and GenAI evaluation.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Curated zone with schema enforcement\n- Time-travel reproducible training snapshots\n- Feature PIT joins tested in CI\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Training on mutable lake paths without versions\n- Online features computed differently than offline\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
