    "id": 86,
    "category": "GenAI",
    "title": "ML Q&A: Model training, deployment, monitoring, registry, and drift",
    "tags": [
      "Model Training",
      "Model Deployment",
      "Model Monitoring",
      "Model Registry",
      "Drift Detection"
    ],
    "excerpt": "MLOps lifecycle Q&A from training through registry, deploy, monitor, and drift response. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-model-lifecycle",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:28.607Z",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "content": "## Scope\nMLOps lifecycle aligned with [MLflow](https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/) registry concepts and production monitoring norms.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **ML Q&A: Model training, deployment, monitoring, registry, and drift** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((ML Q&A: Model training, deployment, monito))\n    Model Training\n    Model Deployment\n    Model Monitoring\n    Model Registry\n    Drift Detection\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What belongs in model training discipline?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nReproducible data snapshots, pinned code/deps, tracked hyperparameters, eval suites, and bias/slice metrics—not only leaderboard scores.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. What is a model registry?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nSystem of record for versions, stages (Staging/Production), signatures, and approvals. Promotions should require eval evidence.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What is model deployment?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nServing a specific artifact behind an API/batch job with rollout strategy (canary/shadow), resource limits, and rollback.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. What is model monitoring?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nTrack service health (latency/errors) and ML health (prediction distribution, calibration, groundedness for GenAI, feedback labels).\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. What is drift detection?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nDetect shifts in input data or prediction behavior vs training/reference. Drift is a signal to investigate—not auto-retrain blindly.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Official references\n- [MLflow](https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/)\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nMLflow-style registry stages, reproducible training, monitored serving, drift as investigation signal. GenAI adds prompt/index versions to the same promotion story. Pair registry with serving (Ray Serve/BentoML/cloud endpoints) rather than collapsing both into one unreplicable process.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nLifecycle discipline is how MLOps differs from a clever notebook.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Pinned data + code + deps for every training run\n- Promotion requires eval evidence\n- Drift alerts route to owners with playbooks\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Auto-retrain on every drift alert without root cause\n- Production model with no lineage to training data\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
