    "excerpt": "Architecture and paradigm Q&A for CNNs, RNNs, GANs, AutoML, and RL—with when each still matters. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-cnn-rnn-gan-automl-rl",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:28.589Z",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "content": "## Scope\nCore ML architecture literacy expected in AI Architect interviews.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **ML Q&A: CNN, RNN, GANs, AutoML, and Reinforcement Learning** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((ML Q&A: CNN, RNN, GANs, AutoML, and Reinfo))\n    CNN\n    RNN\n    GANs\n    AutoML\n    Reinforcement Learning\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What are CNNs best known for?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nConvolutional Neural Networks excel at grid-like data (images, some time-frequency signals) via local receptive fields and weight sharing.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. Where do RNNs still appear?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nRecurrent nets (LSTM/GRU) modeled sequential dependence before Transformers dominated many NLP tasks. Still seen in some time-series/sensor settings, though temporal Transformers/CNNs often compete.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What are GANs?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nGenerative Adversarial Networks: generator vs discriminator training for realistic samples (images historically). Modern image generation often uses diffusion; understand GANs for literacy and legacy systems.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. What is AutoML?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nAutomated search over models/features/hyperparameters to reduce manual trial-and-error. Useful for baselines; still requires data quality, leakage checks, and human oversight for regulated domains.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. What is Reinforcement Learning?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nLearning policies via reward signals from environment interaction. Relevant to tool-use agents, robotics, and optimization—but reward design and safety constraints are the hard parts.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Self-check\nMap one business problem to CNN, one to RL, one to AutoML tabular search.\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nCNNs: spatial grids. RNNs/LSTM: sequential inductive bias (often replaced by Transformers in NLP). GANs: adversarial generation (diffusion often preferred for images now). AutoML: search over pipelines with human oversight. RL: policy learning from rewards — hard part is reward/safety design for agents/tool use.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nInterview and design conversations still expect CNN/RNN/GAN/RL/AutoML literacy even in a Transformer-first world.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Know when classical DL still beats LLMs (vision pipelines, etc.)\n- Treat AutoML outputs as candidates needing leakage/fairness review\n- For RL/tool agents, specify reward and hard safety constraints\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Forcing RL where supervised learning suffices\n- Shipping AutoML models with silent train/serve skew\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
