    "id": 78,
    "category": "GenAI",
    "title": "GenAI Q&A: Prompt engineering, chaining, evaluation, and guardrails",
    "tags": [
      "Prompt Engineering",
      "Prompt Chaining",
      "Prompt Evaluation",
      "Guardrails",
      "Token Control"
    ],
    "excerpt": "Detailed Q&A on prompt design, chained pipelines, eval harnesses, guardrails, and token/context control. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-prompts-guardrails",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:28.558Z",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "content": "## Scope\nIndustry practice aligned with provider evaluation guides ([OpenAI Evaluation](https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/evaluation)) and production LLMOps norms.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **GenAI Q&A: Prompt engineering, chaining, evaluation, and guardrails** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((GenAI Q&A: Prompt engineering, chaining, e))\n    Prompt Engineering\n    Prompt Chaining\n    Prompt Evaluation\n    Guardrails\n    Token Control\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What is prompt engineering in production?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nVersioned instructions that define role, scope, tools, citation rules, and refusal policy—treated as code: reviewed, tested, and released. Not one-off chat tinkering.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. What is prompt chaining?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nDecomposing a task into sequenced prompts (retrieve → extract → reason → verify → format). Each stage has its own schema and eval. Prefer chaining/graphs over one mega-prompt when steps need different tools or confidence gates.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. How do you evaluate prompts?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nOffline: golden sets, rubrics (correctness, groundedness, safety, format), regression vs previous prompt version. Online: A/B or shadow traffic with guardrail metrics as hard gates. Document hypothesis and abort criteria.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. What are guardrails?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nInput/output controls: PII/PHI filters, allowlisted intents, schema validation, toxicity/jailbreak detection, tool permission boundaries, and human escalation. Guardrails belong in the harness, not only in the system prompt.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. What is token control / context optimization?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nBudget max input/output tokens, summarize history, pin critical facts, drop low-relevance retrieved chunks, and monitor cost per successful task. Context optimization is an engineering problem: retrieval quality + packing strategy + truncation policy.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q6. What is memory management for LLM apps?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nShort-term conversational state vs long-term stores (profiles, prior tickets). Persist structured memories with retention/TTL and access control; never treat the raw chat log as the only memory system.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Official references\n- [OpenAI Evaluation](https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/evaluation) · pair with your chosen framework's prompt versioning (LangSmith/Langfuse/MLflow)\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nTreat system/developer/user layers separately; chain stages with schemas. Bedrock documents Prompt management + Guardrails as first-class platform features; MLflow documents prompt versioning/lineage and LLM-as-judge scorers. Evaluate with golden sets (correctness, groundedness, safety, format). Token/context control: budgets, summarization, packing, truncation.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nPrompts are production code. Ungoverned prompt edits are silent regressions.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Prompt versions in registry with owners\n- Eval gate in CI for prompt PRs\n- Guardrails outside the prompt (harness-level)\n- Document refusal and escalation behavior\n- Monitor cost per successful task\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- One mega-prompt doing retrieve+reason+format+safety\n- Guardrails only as soft instructions the model can ignore\n- No abort criteria for online A/B on prompts\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
