    "id": 77,
    "category": "GenAI",
    "title": "GenAI Q&A: MLflow, BentoML, Ray, Truss, and FastAPI serving",
    "tags": [
      "MLflow",
      "BentoML",
      "Ray",
      "Truss",
      "FastAPI"
    ],
    "excerpt": "Serving and registry Q&A: experiment tracking, packaging, scalable inference, and API edges. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-serving-registry",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:28.552Z",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "content": "## Scope\n[MLflow](https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/), [BentoML](https://docs.bentoml.com/), [Ray Serve](https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/serve/index.html), [FastAPI](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/); Truss as a model-packaging/serve pattern in the same design space.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nRegistry → package → serve → observe.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  Train[Train / build] --> Reg[MLflow registry]\n  Reg --> Pkg[Bento / image]\n  Pkg --> Serve[Ray Serve / FastAPI]\n  Serve --> Mon[Metrics + traces]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Serve stack))\n    Track\n      MLflow\n    Package\n      BentoML\n      Truss\n    Serve\n      Ray Serve\n      FastAPI edge\n    Scale\n      GPU\n      Autoscale caps\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What does MLflow provide for GenAI?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nExperiment tracking, model/prompt registry, packaging flavors (including LangChain/LlamaIndex integrations), evaluation, and tracing hooks so artifacts and lineage are auditable across train/serve cycles.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. What is BentoML for?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nPackage ML/LLM services into deployable Bentos/containers with scalable APIs. Common pattern: BentoML service wrapping vLLM or similar backends, then ship OCI images to Kubernetes.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What is Ray Serve?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nRay's scalable, framework-agnostic online inference library. Documented strengths: composition of models + business logic, autoscaling, multi-GPU/multi-node LLM features (streaming, batching). Runs anywhere Ray runs (laptop, K8s, cloud).\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. Where does FastAPI sit?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nFastAPI is the high-performance Python API layer for request validation, auth, and OpenAPI docs. It is often the edge in front of model workers (Ray/Bento/vLLM), not a replacement for a model server.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. What is Truss in this bank?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nTruss is a model packaging/deployment toolkit (Baseten ecosystem) aimed at turning Python model code into reliable services quickly—same architectural concern as Bento: reproducible build → container → scale.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q6. Recommended production topology?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nTrain/track in MLflow → package (Bento/Truss/custom) → serve (Ray Serve / vLLM / managed cloud) → expose via FastAPI/API gateway → observe with Langfuse/LangSmith + infra metrics.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Official references\n- [MLflow](https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/) · [BentoML](https://docs.bentoml.com/) · [Ray Serve](https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/serve/index.html) · [FastAPI](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/)\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nMLflow: split GenAI (trace/eval/prompts) vs classic ML (tracking/registry/deploy); vendor-neutral LF project. Ray Serve: framework-agnostic composition via DeploymentHandle, FastAPI ingress, LLM streaming/batching/multi-GPU/fractional GPUs; laptop→K8s; serving-focused—not a full lifecycle UI. BentoML: `@bentoml.service` / `@bentoml.api`, adaptive batching, streaming, package to Bentos/OCI; examples include vLLM OpenAI-compatible APIs and LangGraph agents. FastAPI: Starlette+Pydantic, OpenAPI `/docs`, strong ML API edge. Truss occupies the same packaging/deploy niche.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nTraining is worthless without reproducible packaging, a registry of truth, and scalable serving with rollback.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Every production artifact has a registry version + signature\n- Canary/shadow before full traffic\n- Independent autoscaling for retrieval vs generation stages\n- Hard spend/GPU caps\n- Rollback runbook tested quarterly\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Serving from a research machine with no image provenance\n- Expecting Ray Serve alone to replace model registry + experiment tracking\n- No cost metrics alongside latency SLOs\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
