    "level": "beginner",
    "content": "## How to Use Multiple Tabs and New Page Handling Quickly?\n\nThe fastest way to handle multiple tabs and new pages in Playwright is by leveraging the **page.waitForEvent** method. This allows you to listen for new page events and switch contexts seamlessly.\n\nHere???s a simple working example to illustrate this:\n\n```js\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright');\n\n(async () => {\n  const browser = await chromium.launch();\n  const context = await browser.newContext();\n  const page = await context.newPage();\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\n  const [newPage] = await Promise.all([\n    context.waitForEvent('page'),\n    page.click('a[target=\"_blank\"]')\n  ]);\n  await newPage.waitForLoadState();\n  console.log(await newPage.title());\n  await browser.close();\n})();\n```\n\n## How to Multiple Tabs and New Page Handling in Detail?\n\nHandling multiple tabs and new pages effectively involves understanding how Playwright manages browser contexts and pages. When a user action triggers a new tab or page, we can listen for that event and manage it accordingly.\n\nIn the following complete example, we will open a new page, interact with it, and then return to the original page. This demonstrates a typical use case in web automation:\n\n```js\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright');\n\n(async () => {\n  const browser = await chromium.launch();\n  const context = await browser.newContext();\n  const page = await context.newPage();\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\n\n  // Click to open a new page\n  const [newPage] = await Promise.all([\n    context.waitForEvent('page'),\n    page.click('a[target=\"_blank\"]')\n  ]);\n\n  // Wait for loading the new page\n  await newPage.waitForLoadState();\n  console.log(await newPage.title());\n\n  // Interact with the new page\n  await newPage.fill('input[name=\"search\"]', 'Playwright');\n  await newPage.click('button[type=\"submit\"]');\n\n  // Close the new page\n  await newPage.close();\n  await browser.close();\n})();\n```\n\nThis code demonstrates how to create a new context and listen for the new page event. It then interacts with the newly opened page before closing it.\n\n## What is Multiple Tabs and New Page Handling?\n\n**Multiple Tabs and New Page Handling** refers to the ability of web automation tools to manage multiple active pages or tabs within a single browser context. This is essential for simulating real user behavior where users often open links in new tabs.\n\nPlaywright introduced this feature to enhance testing capabilities, allowing developers to simulate complex user interactions. When a new tab is opened, it can be handled independently while still maintaining access to the original page context.\n\nInternally, when a user clicks a link that opens a new tab, Playwright listens for the **page** event associated with the browser context. This allows for the creation of a new **Page** object that can be interacted with just like the original.\n\nMultiple tabs can be particularly useful in scenarios like:\n1. **Monitoring changes** in a web application while interacting with another page.\n2. **Testing workflows** that involve navigating between multiple pages.\n3. **Data extraction** from multiple sources simultaneously.\n\n## When to Use Multiple Tabs and New Page Handling?\n\n- **User Testing**: When testing user workflows that involve opening links in new tabs.\n- **Data Comparison**: When you need to compare data from multiple pages side by side.\n- **Monitoring**: For applications that require monitoring updates across different pages.\n- **Multi-step Transactions**: When simulating complex user transactions that require multiple page interactions.\n- **Form Validation**: Validating forms that may open additional pages for submission or processing.\n\n### Is Multiple Tabs and New Page Handling different from Single Page Handling?\n\nYes, multiple tabs and new page handling differ from single page handling in that it requires additional event management and context switching. In single-page handling, all actions occur within the same page context, whereas multiple tabs involve managing multiple **Page** instances simultaneously.\n\n### Does Multiple Tabs and New Page Handling trigger events?\n\nYes, opening new tabs and pages triggers specific events in Playwright, such as **page** and **dialog** events. This allows developers to hook into these events for custom handling.\n\n## What is the Difference Between page.goto() and page.click()?\n\nThe **page.goto()** method navigates directly to a specified URL, whereas **page.click()** simulates a user clicking on an element, which may lead to a new page or tab.\n\n| Method         | When to Use               | Key Behavior                                           | Speed                  |\n|----------------|---------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------|\n| page.goto()    | Navigate to a URL         | Directly loads the page                                | Fast                   |\n| page.click()   | Simulate user interaction  | Possible new page/tab opening depending on the element | May vary (depends on network)|\n\n## Step by Step Guide with Examples\n\n### Step 1: Launch the Browser\n\nFirst, we need to launch an instance of the browser using Playwright.\n\n```js\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright');\n\n(async () => {\n  const browser = await chromium.launch();\n  // Continue to the next steps...\n})();\n```\n\n### Step 2: Create a New Context\n\nNext, create a new browser context to isolate your sessions.\n\n```js\nconst context = await browser.newContext();\n```\n\n### Step 3: Open a New Page\n\nNow, let's open a new page within our context.\n\n```js\nconst page = await context.newPage();\n```\n\n### Step 4: Navigate to a URL\n\nUse the **goto** method to navigate to your desired webpage.\n\n```js\nawait page.goto('https://example.com');\n```\n\n### Step 5: Open a New Tab\n\nSimulate a user clicking a link that opens a new tab.\n\n```js\nconst [newPage] = await Promise.all([\n  context.waitForEvent('page'),\n  page.click('a[target=\"_blank\"]')\n]);\n```\n\n### Step 6: Interact with the New Page\n\nFinally, interact with the new page as needed.\n\n```js\nawait newPage.fill('input[name=\"search\"]', 'Playwright');\nawait newPage.click('button[type=\"submit\"]');\n```\n\n## Common Patterns and Variations\n\n### Pattern 1: Handling Multiple Tabs in a Loop\n\n```js\nconst pages = await Promise.all([\n  context.newPage"
