    "excerpt": "In this comprehensive guide, you'll learn how to implement soft assertions for non-blocking checks in Playwright. Discover best practices, common patterns, and real-world examples to enhance your testing strategies.",
    "sourceUrl": "",
    "createdAt": "2026-04-03T09:56:37.960Z",
    "level": "beginner",
    "content": "## How to Use Soft Assertions for Non-blocking Checks Quickly?\n\nSoft assertions allow you to check conditions without stopping the execution of your tests. This enables a more fluid testing experience where multiple assertions can be evaluated in a single run.\n\nTo implement a soft assertion, you simply need to use the `expect.soft()` method available in Playwright.\n\n```js\n// Example of a soft assertion in action\nconst { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');\n\ntest('example soft assertion', async ({ page }) => {\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\n  expect.soft(await page.title()).toBe('Example Domain');\n  expect.soft(await page.locator('h1').innerText()).toBe('This domain is for use in illustrative examples');\n});\n```\n\n## How to Soft Assertions for Non-blocking Checks in Detail?\n\nSoft assertions in Playwright are designed to enhance the testing workflow by allowing multiple assertions to be executed without halting the test execution on the first failure. This is crucial in scenarios where you want to gather as much information as possible about failures in a single run.\n\nWhen you use `expect.soft()`, it captures the assertion results and logs them without throwing an error immediately. The test continues to run, and once all assertions are evaluated, a summary of the results is provided at the end.\n\nLet's look at a more comprehensive example:\n\n```js\nconst { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');\n\ntest('comprehensive test with soft assertions', async ({ page }) => {\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\n\n  expect.soft(await page.title()).toBe('Example Domain'); // Soft assertion 1\n  expect.soft(await page.locator('h1').innerText()).toBe('Not the right heading'); // Soft assertion 2\n  expect.soft(await page.locator('p').count()).toBe(2); // Soft assertion 3\n\n  // Final check to report all soft assertions\n  const results = await expect.softResults();\n  console.log(results);\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, three soft assertions are made about the page's title, header text, and paragraph count. If any of these assertions fail, the test will still continue to the end.\n\n## What is Soft Assertions for Non-blocking Checks?\n\nSoft assertions are a powerful feature in testing frameworks like Playwright that allow developers to perform multiple checks without breaking the flow of execution. This capability is particularly beneficial in scenarios where you want to gather comprehensive feedback from a single test run.\n\nWhen an assertion is executed using a soft assertion method, it captures the failed state but does not throw an error right away. Instead, it stores the results and allows the test to complete. This means that developers can see all the places where the test failed, rather than just the first failure encountered.\n\nSoft assertions were introduced to address common limitations in traditional testing methodologies. In many cases, developers were forced to fix one issue before they could see others, leading to inefficient debugging processes. By enabling non-blocking checks, soft assertions have become a staple in modern testing practices, allowing for quicker iterations and more robust test outcomes.\n\nIn essence, soft assertions fit perfectly into the ecosystem of automated tests, providing flexibility and insight into the state of the application under test without the typical interruptions caused by hard failures.\n\n## When to Use Soft Assertions for Non-blocking Checks?\n\n- **UI Validation**: When validating multiple UI components on a page without stopping at the first failure.\n- **Data Integrity Checks**: To ensure that multiple data points are correct in a single operation, reducing the back-and-forth of fixing errors.\n- **Performance Testing**: When running performance benchmarks where you want to report multiple metrics in one go.\n- **Regression Testing**: To gather reports on several aspects of the application rather than dealing with one issue at a time.\n- **Form Validation**: When validating user input across multiple fields to provide a complete feedback loop.\n\n### Is Soft Assertions for Non-blocking Checks different from Hard Assertions?\n\nYes, the primary difference lies in the execution flow. **Soft assertions** do not halt the test execution upon failure, while **hard assertions** will throw an error immediately, stopping the test. This allows soft assertions to gather more context about the state of the application during a single test run.\n\n### Does Soft Assertions for Non-blocking Checks trigger events?\n\nSoft assertions themselves do not trigger events but they can log results for later evaluation. This means that while they don't generate immediate feedback, they provide a comprehensive summary of the test outcomes at the end of execution.\n\n## What is the Difference Between Soft Assertions and Regular Assertions?\n\nSoft assertions and regular assertions differ mainly in their handling of failures. Regular assertions will stop the test immediately upon failure, whereas soft assertions allow the test to continue running even when failures occur. This means you can collect all failures in a single run instead of fixing them one at a time.\n\n| Method               | When to Use                          | Key Behavior                                 | Speed          |\n|---------------------|--------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| Regular Assertions   | Simple tests with critical checks   | Halts on first failure                        | Slower debugging |\n| Soft Assertions      | Complex tests needing multiple checks| Collects failures and logs them for review   | Faster feedback  |\n| Mixed Approach       | Combination of both methods          | Stops for critical failures, continues for others | Balanced       |\n\n## Step by Step Guide with Examples\n\nIn this section, we will walk through a detailed process to set up and implement soft assertions in your Playwright tests.\n\n### Step 1: Set Up Your Playwright Environment\n\nMake sure you have Playwright installed in your project. You can do this by running:\n\n```js\nnpm install @playwright/test\n```\n\n### Step 2: Import Playwright Test Library\n\nBegin your test file by importing the necessary components from Playwright.\n\n```js\nconst { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');\n```\n\n### Step 3: Create a Test Case\n\nDefine a test case where you will utilize soft assertions.\n\n```js\ntest('test case with soft assertions', async ({ page }) => {\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\n});\n```\n\n### Step 4: Implement Soft Assertions\n\nWithin your test case, add soft assertions using `expect.soft()`. This allows the test to continue running regardless of the results.\n\n```js\n  expect.soft(await page.title()).toBe('Example Domain');\n  expect.soft(await page.locator('h1').textContent()).toBe('Incorrect Heading');\n```\n\n### Step 5: Capture Results\n\nAt the end of your test, capture and log the assertion results.\n\n```js\n  const results = await expect.softResults();\n  console.log(results);\n```\n\n### Step 6"
